# How this deck was made (W3C PROV-O): researched card by card from the sources below,
# each card checked against at least two of them, then in the review rounds below.
<#compilation>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T12:00:00.000Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    prov:used <https://www.wikidata.org/> ,
              <https://archive.org/details/SwedishCourseText> ,
              <https://folkets-lexikon.csc.kth.se/folkets/> ,
              <https://svenska.se/saol/> ,
              <https://sprakbanken.se/en/resources/saldo> ;
    prov:wasAssociatedWith <#anton-wiklund> ;
    prov:wasInformedBy <#review-0> ,
                       <#review-1> ,
                       <#review-2> ,
                       <#review-3> ;
    rdfs:comment "Compiled by Anton Wiklund with the help of AI."@en ,
                 "Sammanställd av Anton Wiklund med hjälp av AI."@sv ,
                 "About this record: the comments on this compilation, its review rounds and its sources are condensed from the deck's dossier, the working record kept while the deck was compiled. The dossier's per-card evidence and Wikidata checks, its numbered queries and scripts (its Queries section) and its logs of discrepancies are not part of this release; where the text refers to them or to the dossier, it means that record. The builder is the script that built the deck from the dossier's card list and ran its Wikidata checks. The research, the writing of the cards and the checks were done by an AI agent working for Anton Wiklund, and the three independent reviews below were done by other AI agents; no human has reviewed the cards."@en ,
                 "The cards were checked by machine (live Wikidata and the app's SHACL and DCAT-AP validators) and reviewed by AI agents (see the review records); no human reviewed them."@en ,
                 "Word list. A list of 125 words and phrases for a first Swedish course was drawn up from the library editor's brief, the same brief as the library's other basics decks (numbers 0–20, the tens and 100; days; months; eight colours; eight family members; about twelve greetings and polite phrases; everyday nouns, adjectives and verbs), choosing the most basic word for each concept and following the library's German basics deck for the everyday nouns, adjectives and verbs where the languages allow. No frequency list, textbook word list or phrase-book list was copied. The alternatives considered and set aside are listed under Excluded."@en ,
                 "Wikidata lexemes. SPARQL query 1 fetched every Swedish lexeme (language Q9027) whose lemma is one of the candidates or one of 16 alternatives (mor, far, tala, jobba, adjö, förlåt, morfar, farmor, god dag, pengar and others), with its lexical category, grammatical gender (P5185), senses, English and Swedish glosses and sense items (P5137). Of the 125 words on the cards, 120 have a Swedish lexeme that the card cites; förlåt has only a noun lexeme without senses (L245198), which is not cited, and the lexeme search (API calls 2) found no Swedish lexeme for god morgon, goddag, god natt or hej då, nor for the excluded god kväll (only the Danish goddag, the Swedish idiom goddag yxskaft and the verb hejda). The full JSON of the lexeme chosen for each word was fetched with API calls 3: the numeral lexeme for the numbers (for 1, ett L44971, with en L46773 for the note), the noun lexeme for nouns, the verb lexeme for verbs, the interjection for hej, tack, varsågod, välkommen, ja and nej, and the verb ursäkta (L237930, which has no senses). The lexemes give the gender behind every article (common gender Q1305037: en; neuter Q1775461: ett), the plural in every noun's plural note (the form with plural Q146786, nominative Q131105 and indefinite Q53997857), the present tense of every verb (Q192613) and the neuter and plural forms of the adjectives, except gammal's plural (see Notes). The lexemes that the senses' translation statements (P5972) point to were fetched with API calls 4, and query 5 looked for English, Korean, German, Spanish and French senses that point to the Swedish senses: they give the German, Spanish and French names of the days (and the English ones) and a few English and German words (house, good, do, five, grey; Sohn, alt, lesen). In the review round, API calls 17 fetched the lexemes kompis (L32404), åka (L38450) and aderton (L579173) and the translation targets do (L4000), alt (L343308) and lesen (L1759), and re-read the senses behind the backs and notes: varsågod's gloss \"you're welcome\" (L1132766-S1), komma's \"to come\" (L38134-S1), gå's \"to go\" (L38124-S1) and \"förflytta sig med benkraft\" (on foot, L38124-S3), åka's \"färdas\" (L38450-S1), and the language style informal (P6191 Q901711) on the senses of mamma, pappa, kompis and jobba."@en ,
                 "Wikidata items. The items linked from the chosen senses and items found with the item search (API calls 6) and three label queries (queries 6a–6c: good morning Q19279266, good evening Q19279366, good night Q126010969, farewell Q101044775, 80 Q712467) were fetched with API calls 5 (labels and aliases in Swedish, English, Korean, German, Spanish, French and mul). They give the digit label (mul) and the Swedish alias of every number item, and the English, Korean, German, Spanish and French labels or aliases that most backs follow: the days, the months, the colours, the family members and the everyday things, and several greetings (hej Q12068060, tack Q19279214, välkommen Q13109234, ja Q6452715, nej Q1814990) and the verbs gå (Q19279529) and sova (Q12946962). Where an item's label is not the everyday word (the German Haushund, Hauskatze and Innerortsstraße, the Korean 젖 for milk, the Spanish varón and gato doméstico), the back uses the everyday word instead and the difference is logged in the dossier."@en ,
                 "Phrases from the FSI course. The Internet Archive's search (call 7) found the Foreign Service Institute's Swedish Basic Course (1982); its OCR text was downloaded (call 8) and searched with grep and a Python script that prints the matching lines of its Swedish–English word list (call 9). Unit 1's basic sentences give Goddag (\"Hello (how do you do?)\"), Hej (\"Hi\"), God morgon (\"Good morning\"), God natt (\"Good night\"), Adjö (\"Good-bye\"), tack, Tack så mycket, ja, nej, Ursäkta (\"Excuse (me)\") and Talar du svenska? with their English; Unit 2 gives Förlåt (\"Pardon (me)\") and Ingen orsak, and Unit 3 Hej då (\"Bye\"). Its notes say that goddag is a greeting corresponding to \"Hello\", not a farewell, and that the informal hej is more widely used (Unit 1); that varsågod has two uses, \"here you are\" when handing over something and \"you are welcome\" in answer to \"thank you\" (Unit 1; Unit 12 adds \"please\" when offering something, Unit 13 Varsågod och sitt, \"Please sit down\"); that välkommen has the plural välkomna and \"is never used in the English sense of 'you are welcome'\" (Unit 2); that a quantity precedes the noun with no preposition (ett glas mjölk, Unit 1); that the days (Unit 3) and the months (Unit 5) are not capitalised; that ja tack corresponds to \"yes, please\" and att åka tåg is \"to go by train\" (Unit 13). Unit 1 lists the numbers 0–100 (\"1 en, ett\"), Unit 3's word list gives gammal -t, gamla, Unit 6 writes god dag, Unit 12 gives Tack bra (\"Fine, thanks\"), the Unit 8 word list gives blå, blått, blåa and grå, grått, gråa, and the glossary gives man as \"gentleman, man, husband\" and att jobba as \"colloq. to work\". The course is the content source of the five phrases with no Swedish lexeme (god morgon, goddag, god natt, hej då, förlåt) and of ursäkta's meaning, the second content source of varsågod's meanings, and a content source of the facts in several notes. The OCR text often renders å, ä and ö as a, & or d; the spelling on the cards comes from Wikidata and was checked in SAOL, SALDO and Folkets lexikon."@en ,
                 "Writing the cards. The front is the Swedish word in lower case, as the dictionaries write it (Swedish writes days and months in lower case). Nouns, including the family words, are preceded by their indefinite article, en or ett, as the library's swedish-nouns deck gives them (en dag, ett år), chosen by the lexeme's grammatical gender and confirmed by SAOL's inflection and Folkets lexikon's definite form. The exception is the days and months: their lexemes are nouns of common gender, but they are shown bare, as dictionaries and the FSI course list them, and have no plural note. The mass nouns vatten and mjölk keep the article to show their gender, and their note shows them without one after a measure (ett glas mjölk). Verbs are in the infinitive without att, as the library's swedish-verbs deck gives them (vara, ha), although the FSI course cites verbs with att; the English back (\"to be\") shows that the card is a verb. Adjectives and colours are in the basic (common-gender singular) form, numbers spelled out as words (noll, ett, två …) with the digits on the back, tagged zxx (text in no language). Card ids are the front folded to ASCII in lower case with dashes, as in the swedish-nouns deck (#en-dag, #ett-ar). Every text is tagged with its language: the front sv, the backs and notes en, ko, de, es and fr. French texts have a no-break space before : and inside « » and a narrow no-break space before ; ? and !, as in the library's German basics deck."@en ,
                 "The back gives the meaning in English, Korean, German, Spanish and French, one text per language: for nouns the bare noun, for verbs the infinitive (English \"to …\"), for numbers the digits. Each meaning follows a Wikidata label or alias of the linked item, a lexeme gloss or a sense translation where Wikidata has the everyday word (the builder's checks cover these, see Machine checks): among the verbs, to go, gehen, ir and aller (gå), to sleep, schlafen and dormir (sova), to come (komma's gloss), to do (göra, a sense translation to English do), and German alt (gammal) and lesen (läsa), both sense translations. Otherwise, and so for most Korean texts and most verbs, it is the authors' own rendering, written with the help of AI (an AI agent wrote it, following the renderings of the same concepts in the library's German basics deck, https://solid-memo.com/decks/german-basics/v3.ttl, a CC0 deck by the same creator, where they exist) and not taken from any dictionary. The dossier's per-card evidence says, part by part, which texts follow Wikidata or the FSI course and which are the authors' own. The English meanings were confirmed in Folkets lexikon's English translations and the FSI course; the Korean, German, Spanish and French renderings that do not match a Wikidata label were checked by the AI agent that wrote them and by the language review below. Where Swedish has one word for two English words, both are given (stad: town, city; gå: to go, to walk, the second from the sense \"förflytta sig med benkraft\"; varm: warm, hot); varsågod is rendered by the two uses on which the FSI course and Wikidata agree (here you are; you're welcome)."@en ,
                 "Notes. A short note, in the five back languages, gives what a beginner needs and a content source gives: the plural of every noun with an article (from the lexeme form; not for vatten and mjölk, whose notes instead show them without an article after a measure, from the FSI course), the neuter and plural forms of the adjectives and colours (lexeme forms; the plural gamla from the FSI course's \"gammal -t, gamla\", since the lexeme has gamla only as its definite form), the present tense of every verb (lexeme form), en beside ett for 1 (FSI \"1 en, ett\" and lexeme L46773), etthundra (a Swedish alias of the item 100), lower-case days and months and på måndag (FSI), hej when leaving (sense L246342-S3, \"informal farewell (shortening of 'hej då')\"), goddag as a greeting more formal than hej and written god dag (FSI, which writes both), hejdå (a Swedish alias of farewell Q101044775), tack så mycket and ja tack (FSI), the uses of varsågod (FSI and sense L1132766-S1) and välkommen (FSI), mor and far as the more formal words (Swedish labels of mother Q7560 and father Q7565; the senses of mamma and pappa are marked informal), morfar and farmor (items Q4994791 and Q12433519), the plurals farfäder and mormödrar (lexeme forms), man also meaning husband (FSI word list: \"gentleman, man, husband\"), kompis as the informal word (Swedish alias of friend Q17297777; sense L32404-S1 marked informal), tala (FSI: \"Talar du svenska?\"), läsa also meaning to study (sense L38412-S2, \"studera\"), jobba as the informal word (sense L38723-S1, glossed \"arbeta\" and marked informal; FSI: \"colloq. to work\"), gå as going on foot and åka tåg for going by train (sense L38124-S3; FSI \"att åka tåg to go by train\"), and bra also as an adverb (FSI \"Tack bra\", \"Fine, thanks\"). Notes are the authors' own wording; Folkets lexikon, SAOL and SALDO only confirm them, and no note fact rests on them alone."@en ,
                 "Cross-checking. Every Swedish front was looked up in Folkets lexikon's downloaded Swedish–English XML (call 12: word class, English translations, inflected forms), in SAOL through the search API behind svenska.se (call 13: headword, word class, inflection) and in SALDO through Språkbanken's Karp API v7 (call 14: base form and lemgram). Folkets lexikon has 123 of the fronts as written (god morgon only as the one-word Godmorgon, \"goodmorning\"; not hej då; it has tack only as a noun, \"thanks\"), SAOL 123 as headwords (not god morgon and god natt; it has förlåt only as a noun, a curtain), and SALDO all 125 as base forms (god morgon, god natt and hej då as multiword interjections; förlåt only as a noun; gå, komma and göra, whose many compounds filled the search result, were looked up by their sense ids gå..1, komma..1 and göra..1). Every noun's gender in Wikidata agrees with SAOL's and Folkets lexikon's definite form, and every plural, present tense and adjective form in a note agrees with them. Wiktionary was not used."@en ,
                 "SAOL and SO. Svenska Akademiens ordlista (SAOL) and Svensk ordbok (SO) offer no open licence (the svenska.se footer reads \"© Svenska Akademien\"), so SAOL was used for verification only, through single automated look-ups of each front as the library's other decks did (svenska.se's robots.txt allows all agents); nothing was copied from it into the deck. SO was not consulted."@en ,
                 "Machine checks. The dossier's Wikidata checks are re-run against live Wikidata by the builder: 496 checks on 108 items and 104 cards (the Swedish label or alias of each linked item that matches the front, its English, Korean, German, Spanish and French label or alias that matches a part of the back or the word before a bracket in it, the mul digit label of each number item, and the items behind eight notes; five of the matches ignore case, the German labels Rot, Blau, Weiß, Braun and Willkommen), and 367 checks on 124 lexemes and 120 cards (each card's lexeme: its Swedish lemma, its language, the gender behind each noun's article, and each plural, present-tense, neuter and plural adjective form named in a note, where gammal's plural check matches the lexeme's definite form gamla, the lexeme having no form marked plural; the senses and glosses behind backs and notes, the informal style of four senses, three sense translations, and the numeral lexeme en). Where an item's label does not match the card, no check was added and the difference is logged in the dossier. The built deck was validated with the library's checker (the app's SHACL shapes, DCAT-AP and the library rules)."@en ,
                 "Excluded: god kväll (good evening): Wikidata has no Swedish lexeme for it and no Swedish label on good evening Q19279366, and the FSI course does not give it, so no content source the licence allows gives the phrase; förlåt took its place. Adjö (FSI's \"Good-bye\"), now a formal or old-fashioned farewell; hej då covers goodbye. Mor and far as cards of their own (named in the notes on mamma and pappa), morfar and farmor (named in the notes on farfar and mormor), pengar (money; plural only, so it has no indefinite article), the pronouns du and ni (the FSI course's notes say their use depends on the person addressed), kompis, jobba and tala (named in notes), and the pair veta/känna (both to know). The draft's note on aderton, the older form of arton, was dropped in review: Wikidata gives the form but not that it is old, which only verification sources say."@en ,
                 "Selection: 125 cards: the cardinal numbers 0–20, the tens 30–90 and 100 (29); the seven days of the week; the twelve months; eight colours (red, blue, green, yellow, black, white, grey, brown); eight family members (mamma, pappa, bror, syster, son, dotter, farfar, mormor); twelve greetings and polite phrases (hej, god morgon, goddag, god natt, hej då, tack, varsågod, ursäkta, förlåt, välkommen, ja, nej); 25 everyday nouns (hus, vatten, bröd, mjölk, äpple, bok, bord, stol, dörr, fönster, hund, katt, stad, gata, skola, man, kvinna, barn, vän, hand, dag, natt, år, vecka, arbete); eight adjectives (stor, liten, bra, dålig, ny, gammal, kall, varm); and 16 common verbs in the infinitive (vara, ha, gå, komma, göra, prata, äta, dricka, bo, läsa, skriva, sova, arbeta, köpa, se, säga). The list was chosen by the deck's authors for a first course, not copied from any frequency list, textbook or phrase book. Every word has a Wikidata lexeme except god morgon, goddag, god natt, hej då and förlåt (Wikidata has a Swedish lexeme förlåt only as a noun, without senses), which come from the public-domain FSI course. Swedish grandparents are named by side of the family, so the deck has one of each kind (farfar, mormor) and the notes name the other two. The everyday words mamma, pappa and prata were preferred to the more formal mor, far and tala, which are named in notes. The deck is studied front to back only: the reverse would be ambiguous (good: bra; hello: hej or goddag; to speak: prata or tala)."@en ,
                 "Licensing: The deck is CC0 1.0. Its content comes from two sources whose terms allow any reuse: Wikidata's lexemes and items (CC0 1.0: the Swedish lemmas, genders and inflected forms, glosses, language styles, sense translations, and the English, Korean, German, Spanish, French and digit labels the backs follow) and the Foreign Service Institute's Swedish Basic Course (1982), a work of the United States Government with no copyright protection under 17 U.S.C. § 105 (the five phrases with no Swedish lexeme, ursäkta's meaning, varsågod's two uses, and the facts behind several notes). Section 105 applies in the United States; what the deck takes from the course is the Swedish form and English meaning of standard greetings and a few facts of usage, which are not protectable expression anywhere, so the deck's CC0 dedication does not depend on § 105 applying outside the US. The selection of words is the authors' own. The meanings not taken from Wikidata are the authors' own renderings, written with the help of AI; several of the English ones are the same short stock glosses that Folkets lexikon gives (town, city; warm, hot; to go, to walk; to do, to make; sorry). Single words and fixed greetings such as these or \"good night\" and \"bonne nuit\" are facts of usage, not protectable expression, so this does not carry Folkets lexikon's licence into the deck. Folkets lexikon (CC BY-SA 2.5), SALDO (CC BY 4.0) and SAOL (no open licence) were used for verification only: no definition, example, comment or list was taken from them. So no source requires attribution or share-alike for the deck, although all are credited in its provenance. The renderings that follow the library's German basics deck (v3) follow a CC0 deck by the same creator. Quotations from Folkets lexikon, SALDO or SAOL in the dossier are not covered by the deck's CC0 dedication: they remain under their own licences or rights (CC BY-SA 2.5; CC BY 4.0; © Svenska Akademien); quotations from the FSI course are in the public domain."@en .

<#review-0>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Sources, licences and machine checks"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards: every Swedish form, article, plural, adjective form, present tense, meaning and note against the content sources (Wikidata, the FSI course) and the verification sources (Folkets lexikon, SAOL, SALDO); the licences of all five sources; the builder's Wikidata checks, re-run against live Wikidata on 10 October 2026. Done by the AI agent that compiled the deck, with scripts; a machine and self check, not an independent or human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: Every card has a content source: 123 cite Wikidata (a lexeme, an item or both) and the five phrases with no Swedish lexeme, and ursäkta's meaning, come from the FSI course. The re-run of the Wikidata checks for every card that cites Wikidata passed in full: 496 checks on 108 items and 104 cards, and 367 checks on 124 lexemes and 120 cards (the counts after the changes of review round 1). Folkets lexikon, SAOL and SALDO agree with every front they have (see Cross-checking), and the library's checker found the deck valid. The licence evidence of each source was read from its own page or file (Wikidata:Licensing, the Internet Archive item's metadata and the course's title page and preface, Folkets lexikon's about page, SALDO's resource page, the svenska.se footer). Open points left for the reviews: the Korean, German, Spanish and French renderings that no Wikidata label matches had been checked by no second reader; the Korean label of Q1814990 is 아니오, while the card uses the standard spelling 아니요. This check also said that the FSI course disagreed with Wikidata's gloss of varsågod (\"you're welcome\"); review 1 showed that the course gives that use too, and the card was corrected."@en .

<#review-1>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Factual accuracy"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards: the spelling and form of every front, every article and gender, every plural, adjective form and present tense in a note, and every meaning in English, Korean, German, Spanish and French; the 248 Wikidata entities the dossier cites, fetched live on 10 October 2026 and re-checked by script (lemmas, language, word class, gender, sense items, every label or alias quoted for a back, the digit labels and the aliases behind notes), the sense translations re-queried with SPARQL, the FSI quotations looked up in the course's OCR text, and spot checks in Folkets lexikon. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent, with scripts; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No Wikidata claim failed, and the numbers, days, months, colours, family words, articles, plurals and verb forms were found correct. One error: varsågod left out a meaning that both content sources give, and the record said the FSI course disagreed with Wikidata's gloss \"you're welcome\", when the course's Unit 1 note gives both uses (\"here you are\" when handing over something, \"you are welcome\" in answer to \"thank you\"); fixed: the back is now \"here you are, you're welcome\" in English with matching texts in the other languages, the note adds the reply to tack, and the record says the sources agree. Four warnings, all resolved: the note \"Also aderton\" presented an archaic form as an equal variant (the note was removed, since only verification sources say the form is old); the description and record claimed an article for every noun and a plural for every countable one, while the days and months are bare and farfar and mormor had no plural (the exception is now stated in the description and record, and farfar and mormor have the plurals farfäder and mormödrar from their lexemes); the record said every verb's back was the authors' own, while Wikidata gives to go, to sleep, to come, to do, alt and lesen (now cited and checked); and the per-card evidence did not say which parts of six backs were the authors' own (the evidence now attributes every part). Five suggestions: \"More formal: mor\" and \"far\" was applied, with the informal style of the senses of mamma and pappa as its source, and declined for tala, since no content source gives prata's register; the dossier's Folkets lexikon entries for god morgon (it has Godmorgon) and goddag were corrected; the French ja tack is now \"oui, s’il vous plaît\"; and vatten and mjölk now have a note showing them without an article after a measure (ett glas mjölk, from the FSI course)."@en .

<#review-2>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Language, translation and language tags"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: All 125 cards and the deck metadata: every Swedish front, plural, adjective form and present tense; every back and note in English, Korean, German, Spanish and French for meaning, dictionary form, naturalness and typography; the titles, descriptions in all six languages and keywords; the language tags of every text, checked by script; compared with the library's german-basics v3, french-basics v2, swedish-nouns v2 and swedish-verbs v2, and gå, åka and varsågod looked up in live Wikidata. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: No errors: the Swedish forms and every language tag were found correct. Four warnings, all resolved: the French texts had plain spaces before : ; ? ! and inside « » (now a no-break space before : and inside « », a narrow no-break space before ; ? !, as in german-basics v3); \"mots en ett\" read as \"words ending in -ett\" (now \"noms de genre ett\", and in Spanish \"sustantivos de género ett\"); gå's \"to go\" had no caveat (the note now says it is going on foot and that going by train is åka tåg, from Wikidata L38124-S3 and the FSI course); and varsågod's \"please\" could be taken for the word used in requests (\"please\" was removed from the back; the proposed line \"not used to ask for something\" was not added, as no content source states it). Suggestions applied: varsågod in Korean (천만에요 in place of 어서 하세요) and its German note; the French note on hej; 안녕히 계세요 added for hej då; \"hola, buenos días\" for goddag; \"désolé(e)\" for förlåt; the du forms in the prata note; bra also as an adverb (Tack bra, from the FSI course); a note on vatten and mjölk; 어휘소 in the Korean description and the German description's wording. Declined: \"hello, good day\" for goddag (the English follows the FSI course's \"Hello (how do you do?)\", which fits a formal greeting); lilla for liten (the notes give only indefinite forms, as for every adjective); aderton as \"older, formal\" (the note was removed, see review 1); the mixed Korean colour forms and the att before Swedish verbs in other decks, which follow library decks and are a question for the whole library."@en .

<#review-3>
    a prov:Activity ;
    prov:endedAtTime "2026-10-10T00:00:00Z"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    rdfs:label "Licensing, attribution and documentation"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Scope: The licence evidence of all five sources, fetched live on 10 October 2026 (Wikidata:Licensing, the Internet Archive item's metadata and OCR text, Folkets lexikon's about page and XML header, SALDO's resource page, svenska.se and its robots.txt); the CC0 decision, the attribution and every claim about review; every card's evidence (the 244 cited Wikidata entities fetched live and 685 checks re-run, the FSI facts behind the notes found in the OCR text, the unit locators against its unit headers) and the library's conventions. Done by an AI agent working independently of the compiling agent, with scripts; not a human review."@en ,
                 "Outcome: The CC0 decision, the licence evidence and licence IRIs, prov:wasDerivedFrom, the attribution and the absence of any claim of human review were confirmed. Five warnings, all fixed: the plurals blå and grå rested only on SAOL and Folkets lexikon (the notes now give only blåa and gråa, which Wikidata and the FSI course give); gammal's plural gamla was credited only to SAOL and Folkets lexikon (now to the FSI course's \"gammal -t, gamla\", and the record says that the lexeme check matches the definite form); the register of kompis had no cited content source (now sense L32404-S1, marked informal, with a check); the per-card evidence credited Wikidata for whole backs of which part was the authors' own (now attributed part by part); and four FSI unit numbers were wrong (the days and \"Bye\" are in Unit 3, the months in Unit 5, god dag in Unit 6; corrected in the record and dossier). Six suggestions, all applied: the miscounted alternatives, \"every verb\" (now \"most verbs\", with komma's English gloss cited), a sentence on the stock glosses shared with Folkets lexikon, the list of OCR corrections, the library's German basics deck named as the model for the renderings, and these review records."@en .

<https://www.wikidata.org/>
    dcterms:title "Wikidata (Swedish lexemes and items)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Wikidata contributors (Wikimedia Foundation)" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Wikidata:Licensing (read with action=raw) — \"All structured data (i.e. the main, Property, Lexeme, and EntitySchema namespaces) is released into the public domain under Creative Commons Zero.\" Content source: the Swedish lemmas, genders and forms, glosses, sense translations, and the labels and aliases the backs and several notes follow."@en .

<https://archive.org/details/SwedishCourseText>
    dcterms:title "Swedish Basic Course (Foreign Service Institute, 1982), student text as scanned on the Internet Archive (item \"Swedish Basic Course Text\")" ;
    dcterms:creator "Ingrid Beach, Anne-Marie Carnemark, Margareta Feller and Margareta Weyl, with the editorial assistance of Allen I. Weinstein; Foreign Service Institute, U.S. Department of State" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "A work of the United States Government: the title page reads \"FOREIGN SERVICE INSTITUTE U.S. DEPARTMENT OF STATE 1982\", the preface says \"This book was developed entirely at the Foreign Service Institute in Washington\" by \"the Swedish instructional staff\", and \"To the Student\" says it \"has been written especially for the future U.S. diplomat in Sweden\" (quoted with the OCR errors corrected, e.g. \"Thts\", \"tn\", \"instruecttonal\"); the text carries no copyright notice. 17 U.S.C. § 105 (https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/17/105): \"Copyright protection under this title is not available for any work of the United States Government\". The Internet Archive item's metadata (https://archive.org/metadata/SwedishCourseText) gives creator \"Foreign Service Institute\" and licenseurl \"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/mark/1.0/\". Recorded here with the Public Domain Mark: free of known copyright. Content source: the phrases god morgon, goddag, god natt, hej då and förlåt with their meaning, ursäkta's meaning, varsågod's two uses, and facts in several notes."@en .

<https://folkets-lexikon.csc.kth.se/folkets/>
    dcterms:title "Folkets lexikon (Swedish–English)" ;
    dcterms:creator "Viggo Kann and Joachim Hollman (Algoritmica HB) and the contributors of Folkets lexikon; hosted by KTH" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://folkets-lexikon.csc.kth.se/folkets/om.html — \"Både engelsk-svenska lexikonet och svensk-engelska lexikonet kan laddas ner i ett eget XML-format under Distributed Creative Commons Attribution-Share Alike 2.5 Generic license.\"; the downloaded file folkets_sv_en_public.xml states license=\"http://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.5/\". Used for verification only: nothing was taken from it."@en .

<https://svenska.se/saol/>
    dcterms:title "Svenska Akademiens ordlista (SAOL), 14th edition, on svenska.se" ;
    dcterms:creator "Svenska Akademien" ;
    rdfs:comment "https://svenska.se/ — the site footer reads \"© Svenska Akademien\"; no open licence is offered for the dictionary content, so SAOL is treated as all rights reserved. Used for verification only, through single look-ups of each front in the search API behind svenska.se (robots.txt: \"User-agent: * Allow: /\"): nothing was taken from it."@en .

<https://sprakbanken.se/en/resources/saldo>
    dcterms:title "SALDO (Swedish Associative Thesaurus), development version through Språkbanken's Karp API (v7), queried 2026-10-10" ;
    dcterms:creator "Lars Borin, Markus Forsberg, Lennart Lönngren; Språkbanken Text, University of Gothenburg" ;
    dcterms:license <https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/> ;
    rdfs:comment "https://sprakbanken.se/en/resources/saldo: the resource's metadata table gives \"saldo.xml lexicon (LMF) 70.98 MB 2017-09-19 CC-BY-4.0\"; the page's description also says \"All components are released under a Creative Commons Attribute-Share Alike license.\" Either way SALDO is used for verification only: nothing was taken from it."@en .

